I READ the test (internal/handlers/whatif/spending_keep_current_label_test.go).
It is NOT weaker than its name: fixture = qualifying current card (Kind
"current", Qualifies true, Baseline true) + planned-8500 + flex-9000 with
RecommendationIDs ["planned-8500","flex-9000"]. It then asserts, on the
rendered body:
  - exactly 5 buttons matching data-spending-apply="..." (t.Fatalf otherwise);
  - labels["current"] must be len 1 AND == "Keep current plan" (line 52-53);
  - for planned-8500 and flex-9000: len 2 AND got[0]=="Apply this option"
    AND got[1]=="Apply" (lines 57-60) — i.e. card label then frontier-row label;
  - the label is a visible text node, NOT an aria-label override
    (rejects `aria-label="Keep current plan"`, line 64);
  - strings.Count(body,"Keep current plan") == 1 exactly (line 67).
The label is extracted by stripping tags from the button's inner HTML, so it
asserts the RENDERED visible string, not a template fragment.

